WEBVTT

NOTE
SYNTHETIC BENCHMARK FIXTURE. Fictional interview for the Verbatim eval set (Ledgerly study).
Participant p5: Grace Whitfield, Controller, Ironbridge Mechanical (commercial HVAC & plumbing subcontractor), 210 employees. Not a real person.

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:06.680
<v Interviewer>Hi Grace, thanks for joining. Are you all set?

00:00:07.795 --> 00:00:16.248
<v Grace Whitfield>I am. Let me just — sorry, I've got the window open and there's a jackhammer across the street.

00:00:16.455 --> 00:00:19.044
<v Grace Whitfield>Let me close it. Okay, better?

00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:25.802
<v Interviewer>Much better. We'll record this, if that's alright. It should take about fifteen minutes.

00:00:26.241 --> 00:00:27.722
<v Grace Whitfield>Yes, that's fine.

00:00:28.335 --> 00:00:30.808
<v Interviewer>Great. Tell me about your role.

00:00:31.948 --> 00:00:40.290
<v Grace Whitfield>I'm the controller at Ironbridge Mechanical. We're a commercial HVAC and plumbing sub, about two hundred and ten people.

00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:49.145
<v Grace Whitfield>Most of them are in the field, so, you know, foremen, technicians, apprentices. The office is maybe thirty of us.

00:00:50.411 --> 00:00:54.212
<v Interviewer>And Ledgerly — how long have you been using it?

00:00:55.477 --> 00:00:59.067
<v Grace Whitfield>We signed in late September. So, about six weeks?

00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:06.528
<v Grace Whitfield>We gave cards to the foremen first, about forty of them, and then the project managers.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.212
<v Interviewer>Why Ledgerly?

00:01:09.265 --> 00:01:17.709
<v Grace Whitfield>The foremen buy materials at the supply house constantly. Fittings, a box of screws, a saw blade, whatever's missing that morning.

00:01:17.966 --> 00:01:26.538
<v Grace Whitfield>Before, they'd use a house account at the supply house, or their own money, and then I'd get a shoebox of receipts.

00:01:26.668 --> 00:01:29.338
<v Grace Whitfield>I'm not exaggerating. An actual shoebox.

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:33.244
<v Interviewer>How have the first six weeks gone?

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:38.044
<v Grace Whitfield>Um. I'd say we're not where I hoped.

00:01:38.234 --> 00:01:42.088
<v Grace Whitfield>Some of it's us. Some of it's the product.

00:01:42.986 --> 00:01:44.764
<v Interviewer>What's the "us" part?

00:01:45.132 --> 00:01:53.158
<v Grace Whitfield>Oh, we rushed it. We rolled it out the same week as a big safety audit, so nobody had any attention left.

00:01:53.265 --> 00:02:00.879
<v Grace Whitfield>And our foremen are, um, let's say they're set in their ways. Some of them have been doing this thirty years.

00:02:01.893 --> 00:02:04.007
<v Interviewer>Let's start wherever you like.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:11.472
<v Grace Whitfield>Okay, the field. The idea was the foreman buys something, snaps the receipt, it's attached to the job.

00:02:11.623 --> 00:02:15.849
<v Grace Whitfield>Except a lot of our job sites are half-built buildings.

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:23.343
<v Grace Whitfield>You're three floors down in a parking structure, or inside a steel frame, and the phone just doesn't connect.

00:02:23.433 --> 00:02:33.402
<v Grace Whitfield>So they snap it, and it sits in this "sending" state, and then at some point it decides it's failed and drops it.

00:02:33.532 --> 00:02:41.149
<v Grace Whitfield>I've had foremen show me the screen — it's just an empty card that says "receipt missing."

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:43.494
<v Interviewer>How often?

00:02:43.972 --> 00:02:51.667
<v Grace Whitfield>Hard to say exactly. I'd guess a quarter of field receipts in October needed some kind of follow-up.

00:02:51.919 --> 00:02:55.329
<v Grace Whitfield>That's a lot of texting foremen at six a.m.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.788
<v Interviewer>What do the foremen think?

00:02:59.718 --> 00:03:03.879
<v Grace Whitfield>They think I'm nagging them. [laughs] Which, I am.

00:03:03.967 --> 00:03:07.901
<v Grace Whitfield>But they did the right thing, they took the picture.

00:03:08.693 --> 00:03:12.979
<v Interviewer>Okay. You said some of it's the product. What else?

00:03:14.234 --> 00:03:17.462
<v Grace Whitfield>The limits on the cards. Oh, boy.

00:03:17.744 --> 00:03:25.013
<v Grace Whitfield>So I set up limits by role. Foremen get two thousand a month, PMs get five. Simple, right?

00:03:25.192 --> 00:03:32.295
<v Grace Whitfield>Except then there's a per-purchase limit that defaults to something, I think five hundred, which nobody told me about.

00:03:32.588 --> 00:03:38.708
<v Grace Whitfield>So a foreman tries to buy a thousand-dollar pump at the supply house and it bounces.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:46.108
<v Grace Whitfield>And the foreman looks in the app and sees "two thousand available." So he thinks the card is broken.

00:03:46.832 --> 00:03:49.368
<v Interviewer>How did you work it out?

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:59.041
<v Grace Whitfield>I called our rep. He walked me through a screen I'd never seen, where the per-purchase thing is hidden under, like, "advanced controls."

00:03:59.292 --> 00:04:04.404
<v Grace Whitfield>I'm a controller. Controls are my whole job. I didn't find it.

00:04:04.525 --> 00:04:09.057
<v Grace Whitfield>And then there's whether the limit is per card or per person.

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:21.297
<v Grace Whitfield>One of our PMs has a physical card and a virtual card for online orders, and I still don't know if they share the five thousand or if he's got ten.

00:04:22.064 --> 00:04:24.460
<v Interviewer>Did you get an answer?

00:04:25.345 --> 00:04:30.045
<v Grace Whitfield>I got an answer. I'm not confident it's the right answer. [laughs]

00:04:30.662 --> 00:04:33.877
<v Interviewer>Fair. Let's talk about the books side.

00:04:34.372 --> 00:04:37.200
<v Grace Whitfield>Yes. So we're on QuickBooks Online.

00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:44.506
<v Grace Whitfield>We track everything by job, using classes. That's how we know if a job's making money.

00:04:44.716 --> 00:04:50.154
<v Grace Whitfield>Ledgerly's sync is supposed to send each expense over with the job as the class.

00:04:50.334 --> 00:05:00.567
<v Grace Whitfield>In October, about half came through with no class at all. And a bunch landed in the wrong expense account — materials going into "office supplies."

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.542
<v Interviewer>How did you catch it?

00:05:04.554 --> 00:05:10.900
<v Grace Whitfield>Our job cost report looked insane. A job that should've had eight thousand in materials showed two.

00:05:11.205 --> 00:05:13.153
<v Grace Whitfield>So I started digging.

00:05:13.425 --> 00:05:19.593
<v Grace Whitfield>And there were duplicates too. Some expenses synced twice. Same amount, same date, two entries in QuickBooks.

00:05:20.677 --> 00:05:23.281
<v Interviewer>What did fixing that look like?

00:05:23.898 --> 00:05:30.274
<v Grace Whitfield>My AP clerk, Rosa, and I spent the better part of three days going line by line.

00:05:30.397 --> 00:05:40.738
<v Grace Whitfield>We've told our PMs not to trust job cost reports until we've cleaned up October. That's not great when you're trying to bid on new work.

00:05:42.023 --> 00:05:44.818
<v Interviewer>Have you heard from Ledgerly about it?

00:05:45.413 --> 00:05:57.171
<v Grace Whitfield>They said the class mapping has to be set per category and then again per job, and that if you set it on one and not the other, the category wins.

00:05:57.428 --> 00:06:01.049
<v Grace Whitfield>I'd set it on the job. Who knew.

00:06:02.021 --> 00:06:05.173
<v Interviewer>Do you use the Ledgerly mobile app yourself?

00:06:05.875 --> 00:06:10.974
<v Grace Whitfield>I tried. Honestly, it's unusable for me. I couldn't find anything I needed.

00:06:11.175 --> 00:06:17.443
<v Grace Whitfield>There's a tab that says "activity," and a tab that says "spend," and I went in circles.

00:06:17.738 --> 00:06:25.359
<v Grace Whitfield>I'm not a technophobe. I've run three ERPs. But the app is just — the navigation doesn't make sense to me.

00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:32.002
<v Grace Whitfield>So I do everything on the laptop now and I've deleted it off my phone.

00:06:33.085 --> 00:06:35.563
<v Interviewer>Is that common in your office?

00:06:36.013 --> 00:06:41.990
<v Grace Whitfield>The PMs mostly use the laptop too. The foremen have to use the phone, obviously.

00:06:42.175 --> 00:06:46.841
<v Grace Whitfield>And they don't love it, but that's mostly the sending thing.

00:06:48.084 --> 00:06:52.342
<v Interviewer>Three ERPs — what were those like, compared to this?

00:06:53.564 --> 00:06:59.383
<v Grace Whitfield>Oh, much worse in some ways. One of them needed a consultant to change a dropdown.

00:06:59.532 --> 00:07:05.385
<v Grace Whitfield>But they were boring and predictable, and in my job boring is a compliment.

00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:09.023
<v Interviewer>How did you train the foremen?

00:07:09.578 --> 00:07:20.066
<v Grace Whitfield>We did it at the Monday safety meeting. Ten minutes, a projector that didn't want to work, and a lot of guys holding their phones at arm's length.

00:07:20.292 --> 00:07:30.912
<v Grace Whitfield>I made a little laminated card with three steps on it. Buy, snap, pick the job. Some of them have it zip-tied to their clipboard now.

00:07:31.692 --> 00:07:33.076
<v Interviewer>Did that help?

00:07:34.044 --> 00:07:38.690
<v Grace Whitfield>With the steps, yes. People know what they're supposed to do.

00:07:38.838 --> 00:07:47.269
<v Grace Whitfield>It just doesn't help when the thing doesn't go through. You can't laminate your way out of that. [laughs]

00:07:47.712 --> 00:07:53.310
<v Interviewer>Let me take a short detour. What else is going on at Ironbridge this fall?

00:07:54.420 --> 00:08:01.257
<v Grace Whitfield>Rain. So much rain. We've had three weeks of delays on a hospital job because it won't stop raining.

00:08:01.597 --> 00:08:08.719
<v Grace Whitfield>And on a personal note, my daughter's doing college applications, so our kitchen table is covered in essays.

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:10.686
<v Interviewer>That's a lot.

00:08:11.335 --> 00:08:14.354
<v Grace Whitfield>It's fine. It's fall. Everything's due at once.

00:08:15.085 --> 00:08:17.270
<v Interviewer>How's the hospital job otherwise?

00:08:18.154 --> 00:08:25.040
<v Grace Whitfield>It's a good job. Big, complicated, lots of change orders, which is where we make or lose money.

00:08:25.149 --> 00:08:36.246
<v Grace Whitfield>That's actually why the job costing matters so much to me right now. If materials aren't landing on that job, I can't tell if we're fine or if we're bleeding.

00:08:37.287 --> 00:08:41.430
<v Interviewer>Who else in the office is affected by the sync problems?

00:08:42.418 --> 00:08:51.874
<v Grace Whitfield>The PMs, mostly. They use job cost to decide what to order next. And our owner, who reads the reports every Friday with a red pen.

00:08:52.313 --> 00:08:58.216
<v Interviewer>[laughs] Is there a story from the field that sums up the month for you?

00:08:59.209 --> 00:09:09.595
<v Grace Whitfield>Hmm. One of our foremen, Luis, brought me his phone and a crumpled receipt in a sandwich bag. He said, "I don't trust it, so here's both."

00:09:09.871 --> 00:09:16.991
<v Grace Whitfield>That's the month, honestly. People doing the new thing and the old thing at the same time.

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:22.635
<v Interviewer>Okay, back to Ledgerly. If you could fix one thing?

00:09:23.833 --> 00:09:31.473
<v Grace Whitfield>The sync. Get the job onto every expense, every time. If I can't trust job costing, the cards don't matter.

00:09:31.615 --> 00:09:38.577
<v Grace Whitfield>And then make the limit screen show all the limits, not one of them. Put them in one place.

00:09:39.175 --> 00:09:42.404
<v Interviewer>Got it. Anything you want to add?

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:47.270
<v Grace Whitfield>Just that I think it could be great.

00:09:47.497 --> 00:09:56.262
<v Grace Whitfield>The foremen being able to buy what they need without calling me — when it works, that's a real change.

00:09:56.389 --> 00:09:59.116
<v Grace Whitfield>I just need it to work.

00:09:59.835 --> 00:10:02.991
<v Interviewer>Thank you, Grace. This was really helpful.

00:10:03.545 --> 00:10:04.516
<v Grace Whitfield>Of course.
